WEBVTT

00:00:00.418 --> 00:00:05.776
Introducing IdeaAMBIG: a benchmark for finding implementation-critical gaps in research-idea specifications.

00:00:05.926 --> 00:00:07.719
Before an AI system writes the code,

00:00:07.869 --> 00:00:09.324
can it tell what still needs clarification?

00:00:10.309 --> 00:00:12.516
A research idea can have a novel objective,

00:00:12.547 --> 00:00:13.483
convincing motivation,

00:00:13.708 --> 00:00:14.930
and a promising mechanism.

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.345
But a method-defining decision may remain unresolved.

00:00:17.503 --> 00:00:19.861
Different unsupported assumptions lead to different methods.

00:00:20.018 --> 00:00:21.678
That is why codification readiness matters.

00:00:22.676 --> 00:00:24.088
Consider a mixture-of-experts model.

00:00:24.238 --> 00:00:27.525
A specification says to match a dense baseline to its activated

00:00:27.525 --> 00:00:30.135
parameter count. Does that count include only the experts used for

00:00:30.135 --> 00:00:31.706
each token, or every expert?

00:00:31.856 --> 00:00:32.998
The answer changes the baseline,

00:00:33.148 --> 00:00:34.459
not just an engineering detail.

00:00:35.451 --> 00:00:37.823
IdeaAMBIG contains 660 instances:

00:00:38.007 --> 00:00:42.227
163 real-world gaps and 497 controlled synthetic gaps.

00:00:42.385 --> 00:00:45.736
Real gaps come from resolved GitHub issues and reproducibility reports.

00:00:45.886 --> 00:00:48.512
Synthetic gaps alter one detail in a ready reference.

00:00:48.645 --> 00:00:51.911
Every retained instance undergoes two-stage human verification.

00:00:52.909 --> 00:00:55.677
Each main-benchmark instance isolates one target defect.

00:00:55.835 --> 00:00:57.709
The taxonomy distinguishes ambiguity,

00:00:57.885 --> 00:01:01.243
incompleteness, and inconsistency, with ten categories in total.

00:01:01.401 --> 00:01:04.819
Each underspecified input has a counterpart that resolves only its target

00:01:04.819 --> 00:01:05.223
defect.

00:01:06.268 --> 00:01:08.694
First, readiness assessment. Given one specification,

00:01:08.853 --> 00:01:11.322
the model predicts READY or NOT READY and explains why.

00:01:11.472 --> 00:01:13.467
Each specification is presented independently,

00:01:13.643 --> 00:01:15.089
without showing its paired counterpart.

00:01:16.084 --> 00:01:19.397
Second, defect localization. Given an underspecified specification,

00:01:19.538 --> 00:01:22.724
the model must recover the unresolved decision and both taxonomy labels.

00:01:22.866 --> 00:01:24.759
Naming a plausible category is not enough:

00:01:24.926 --> 00:01:27.259
the diagnosis must identify the actual blocker.

00:01:28.285 --> 00:01:30.127
Third, clarification action generation.

00:01:30.294 --> 00:01:31.933
Here the annotated defect is supplied.

00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:34.652
The model proposes a targeted question or an evidence-seeking action,

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:37.733
specifying the information needed to resolve the gap without inventing an

00:01:37.733 --> 00:01:38.088
answer.

00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:44.457
Readiness Macro-F1 reaches 67.5 on real-world gaps versus 86.4 on synthetic

00:01:44.457 --> 00:01:47.568
gaps. Yet 31 percent of underspecified inputs are accepted;

00:01:47.726 --> 00:01:49.794
34 percent of ready inputs are rejected.

00:01:49.952 --> 00:01:52.441
Reason grounding reaches only 0.36.

00:01:53.418 --> 00:01:55.639
Level-1 accuracy reaches 60.1;

00:01:55.856 --> 00:02:00.542
Level-2 accuracy, 25.2. But localization accuracy is 16.0,

00:02:00.717 --> 00:02:03.023
and Macro defect recovery just 9.6.

00:02:03.181 --> 00:02:06.778
Without category prediction, localization improves to 40.5 percent.

00:02:06.937 --> 00:02:08.365
Discovering the blocker remains hard.

00:02:09.351 --> 00:02:13.677
Given the blocker, clarification success reaches 80.6 on real-world instances versus

00:02:13.677 --> 00:02:15.653
96.2 on synthetic instances.

00:02:15.812 --> 00:02:18.203
Sufficiency is 80.4; No-Assumption,

00:02:18.252 --> 00:02:22.316
95.7. Remaining failures mainly reflect incomplete clarification.

00:02:23.301 --> 00:02:24.887
On the same 163 instances,

00:02:25.012 --> 00:02:29.094
supplying the blocker raises clarification success from 13.6 to 80.6.

00:02:29.244 --> 00:02:31.876
Sufficiency rises from 8.6 to 80.4.

00:02:32.009 --> 00:02:35.906
No-Assumption barely changes: 96.3 versus 95.7.

00:02:36.056 --> 00:02:37.718
The model is better at acting on a known blocker.

00:02:38.718 --> 00:02:42.191
With oracle resolutions, the ready rate rises from fourteen to ninety-eight

00:02:42.191 --> 00:02:44.683
percent. Completeness rises from thirty to ninety-eight;

00:02:44.765 --> 00:02:47.233
missing-detail recovery, from fourteen to ninety-eight.

00:02:47.384 --> 00:02:48.678
All three gains are significant,

00:02:48.837 --> 00:02:50.561
but not end-to-end agent performance.

00:02:51.549 --> 00:02:54.037
The key finding: discovering the blocker is the bottleneck.

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:55.654
On the same 163 instances,

00:02:55.713 --> 00:02:59.573
supplying it raises clarification success from 13.6 to 80.6 percent.

00:03:00.553 --> 00:03:02.492
With 660 evidence-grounded instances,

00:03:02.633 --> 00:03:04.416
IdeaAMBIG measures three steps:

00:03:04.609 --> 00:03:06.270
assess readiness, discover the blocker,

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:07.619
and obtain the missing information.

00:03:07.786 --> 00:03:12.258
The next priority is reliable gap diagnosis and evidence-grounded clarification before

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:12.563
coding.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:14.838
Find the gap before coding.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:17.333
The paper, code, and benchmark are available.

00:03:17.497 --> 00:03:18.549
Explore IdeaAMBIG.
